Vault Security Best Practices
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Install and initialize HashiCorp Vault with a hardened configuration
Implement Vault access control policies (ACL) for different user roles
Authenticate using both the userpass and AppRole authentication methods
Enable and validate audit logging and the transit encryption engine
Run a scripted security test suite to validate a Vault deployment
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Linux command line operations
Familiarity with JSON and HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) syntax
Understanding of basic cryptographic concepts (encryption, certificates)
Experience with a text editor such as nano or vim
This lab is intended to be performed on a single, self-owned or lab-provisioned Linux VM (Ubuntu/Debian-based) that you are authorized to configure. No pre-installed tools are required; all software is installed during the lab.

Install commands used later in this lab (for reference, executed in Task 1):

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl gpg openssl
Task 1: Install, Configure, and Initialize Vault
Subtask 1.1: Install Vault
# Update system packages
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required dependencies
sudo apt install -y wget unzip curl gpg openssl

# Add HashiCorp GPG key and repository
wget -O- https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list

# Install Vault
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y vault

# Verify installation
vault version
Subtask 1.2: Create Directories and a Dedicated User
sudo mkdir -p /etc/vault.d /opt/vault/data /var/log/vault /var/log/vault/audit

# Create a system user for Vault
sudo useradd --system --home /etc/vault.d --shell /bin/false vault || echo "user already exists"

sudo chown -R vault:vault /etc/vault.d /opt/vault/data /var/log/vault
Subtask 1.3: Generate TLS Certificates
sudo openssl genrsa -out /etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem 2048

sudo openssl req -new -key /etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem -out /etc/vault.d/vault.csr \
  -subj "/C=US/ST=State/L=City/O=Organization/CN=localhost"

sudo openssl x509 -req -in /etc/vault.d/vault.csr -signkey /etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem \
  -out /etc/vault.d/vault-cert.pem -days 365

sudo chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem /etc/vault.d/vault-cert.pem
sudo chmod 600 /etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem
sudo chmod 644 /etc/vault.d/vault-cert.pem
sudo rm /etc/vault.d/vault.csr
Subtask 1.4: Write the Vault Configuration File
sudo tee /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl > /dev/null << 'EOF'
storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address       = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable   = false
  tls_cert_file = "/etc/vault.d/vault-cert.pem"
  tls_key_file  = "/etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem"
}

api_addr     = "https://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"

ui = true
disable_mlock = true
log_level = "INFO"
log_file  = "/var/log/vault/vault.log"
EOF

sudo chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
Subtask 1.5: Create the Systemd Service
sudo tee /etc/systemd/system/vault.service > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[Unit]
Description=HashiCorp Vault
Documentation=https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/
Requires=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
ConditionFileNotEmpty=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=3

[Service]
Type=notify
User=vault
Group=vault
ProtectSystem=full
ProtectHome=read-only
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
SecureBits=keep-caps
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_IPC_LOCK
CapabilityBoundingSet=CAP_SYSLOG CAP_IPC_LOCK
NoNewPrivileges=yes
ExecStart=/usr/bin/vault server -config=/etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
ExecReload=/bin/kill --signal HUP $MAINPID
KillMode=process
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStopSec=30
LimitNOFILE=65536
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
EOF

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable vault
sudo systemctl start vault
sudo systemctl status vault --no-pager
Subtask 1.6: Initialize and Unseal Vault
export VAULT_ADDR='https://127.0.0.1:8200'
export VAULT_SKIP_VERIFY=true   # only acceptable for self-signed certs in this isolated lab

# Initialize Vault (writes unseal keys and root token to a local file)
vault operator init -key-shares=5 -key-threshold=3 > /tmp/vault-init.txt
cat /tmp/vault-init.txt
Save the unseal keys and root token shown in the output. Create a reusable unseal helper script:

cat > /tmp/unseal-vault.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# Unseals Vault using the keys stored in /tmp/vault-init.txt.
# For demonstration only: in production use auto-unseal via a cloud KMS.

export VAULT_ADDR='https://127.0.0.1:8200'
export VAULT_SKIP_VERIFY=true

UNSEAL_KEY_1=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
UNSEAL_KEY_2=$(grep 'Unseal Key 2:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
UNSEAL_KEY_3=$(grep 'Unseal Key 3:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')

if vault status 2>/dev/null | grep -q "Sealed.*true"; then
    echo "Vault is sealed. Unsealing..."
    vault operator unseal "$UNSEAL_KEY_1"
    vault operator unseal "$UNSEAL_KEY_2"
    vault operator unseal "$UNSEAL_KEY_3"
    echo "Vault unsealed."
else
    echo "Vault is already unsealed (or unreachable)."
fi

export ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
vault login -no-print "$ROOT_TOKEN"
echo "Logged in with root token."
EOF

chmod +x /tmp/unseal-vault.sh
/tmp/unseal-vault.sh

# Re-export the root token in the current shell for subsequent commands
export ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
export VAULT_TOKEN=$ROOT_TOKEN

vault status
Deliverable: vault status shows Sealed: false and Initialized: true. You have a running Vault instance, saved unseal keys/root token in /tmp/vault-init.txt, and a reusable /tmp/unseal-vault.sh script.

Task 2: Access Control Policies and Authentication Methods
Subtask 2.1: Create Policies
# Admin policy - full access
cat > /tmp/admin-policy.hcl << 'EOF'
path "*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete", "list", "sudo"]
}
EOF

# Web application policy - read-only on its own secrets
cat > /tmp/webapp-policy.hcl << 'EOF'
path "secret/data/webapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
path "secret/metadata/webapp/*" {
  capabilities = ["list"]
}
path "auth/token/renew-self" {
  capabilities = ["update"]
}
path "auth/token/lookup-self" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF

# Developer policy - full access to dev secrets, read-only on shared secrets
cat > /tmp/developer-policy.hcl << 'EOF'
path "secret/data/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["create", "read", "update", "delete"]
}
path "secret/metadata/dev/*" {
  capabilities = ["list"]
}
path "secret/data/shared/*" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
path "auth/token/renew-self" {
  capabilities = ["update"]
}
path "auth/token/lookup-self" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
EOF

vault policy write admin /tmp/admin-policy.hcl
vault policy write webapp /tmp/webapp-policy.hcl
vault policy write developer /tmp/developer-policy.hcl

vault policy list
Subtask 2.2: Enable Authentication Methods and Create Users
vault auth enable userpass
vault auth enable approle
vault auth list

# Users
vault write auth/userpass/users/alice password=alice123 policies=developer
vault write auth/userpass/users/bob   password=bob123   policies=webapp

# Enable KV v2 at the default secret/ mount (required before writing secrets below)
vault secrets enable -path=secret -version=2 kv
Subtask 2.3: Create and Test an AppRole
# Create an AppRole bound to the webapp policy
vault write auth/approle/role/webapp-role \
    token_policies="webapp" \
    token_ttl=1h \
    token_max_ttl=4h \
    bind_secret_id=true

# Retrieve the role-id
ROLE_ID=$(vault read -field=role_id auth/approle/role/webapp-role/role-id)
echo "Role ID: $ROLE_ID"

# Generate a secret-id
SECRET_ID=$(vault write -f -field=secret_id auth/approle/role/webapp-role/secret-id)
echo "Secret ID: $SECRET_ID"

# Authenticate using the AppRole credentials
APPROLE_TOKEN=$(vault write -field=token auth/approle/login \
    role_id="$ROLE_ID" \
    secret_id="$SECRET_ID")

echo "AppRole token: $APPROLE_TOKEN"

# Confirm the resulting token carries the webapp policy
VAULT_TOKEN=$APPROLE_TOKEN vault token lookup -format=json | grep -A3 policies
Deliverable: the token lookup output for $APPROLE_TOKEN lists "webapp" (and "default") in its policies, proving the AppRole login succeeded and is correctly scoped.

Subtask 2.4: Test User Policies
# Log in as alice (developer) and bob (webapp)
ALICE_TOKEN=$(vault write -field=token auth/userpass/login/alice password=alice123)
BOB_TOKEN=$(vault write -field=token auth/userpass/login/bob password=bob123)

# Alice can write/read dev secrets
VAULT_TOKEN=$ALICE_TOKEN vault kv put secret/dev/app-config username=devuser password=devpass
VAULT_TOKEN=$ALICE_TOKEN vault kv get secret/dev/app-config

# Alice should NOT be able to write webapp secrets
echo "Expect permission denied below:"
VAULT_TOKEN=$ALICE_TOKEN vault kv put secret/webapp/config username=webuser password=webpass

# Root token creates a webapp secret for Bob to read
vault kv put secret/webapp/config username=webuser password=webpass

# Bob can read webapp secrets
VAULT_TOKEN=$BOB_TOKEN vault kv get secret/webapp/config

# Bob should NOT be able to write new webapp secrets
echo "Expect permission denied below:"
VAULT_TOKEN=$BOB_TOKEN vault kv put secret/webapp/newconfig test=value
Deliverable: Alice's write to secret/webapp/config and Bob's write to secret/webapp/newconfig both return a permission denied error from Vault, while Alice's dev-secret operations and Bob's webapp read succeed. This confirms policy enforcement is working as intended.

Task 3: Audit Logging and Transit Encryption
Subtask 3.1: Enable Audit Logging
sudo mkdir -p /var/log/vault/audit
sudo chown vault:vault /var/log/vault/audit

vault audit enable file file_path=/var/log/vault/audit/vault-audit.log
vault audit list
Subtask 3.2: Generate and Inspect Audit Events
vault kv put secret/dev/audit-test data=test-value
vault kv get secret/dev/audit-test
vault kv delete secret/dev/audit-test

# Each line is a JSON object; pretty-print the most recent entries
sudo tail -5 /var/log/vault/audit/vault-audit.log | python3 -m json.tool 2>/dev/null || \
  sudo tail -5 /var/log/vault/audit/vault-audit.log
Deliverable: /var/log/vault/audit/vault-audit.log contains JSON entries with a "type" field (request or response) and a nested "request" object showing the secret/data/dev/audit-test path, confirming audit logging captures real Vault operations.

Subtask 3.3: Configure the Transit Encryption Engine
vault secrets enable transit
vault write -f transit/keys/webapp-key

# Encrypt data
CIPHERTEXT=$(echo -n "sensitive data" | base64 | vault write -field=ciphertext transit/encrypt/webapp-key plaintext=-)
echo "Ciphertext: $CIPHERTEXT"

# Decrypt data
vault write -field=plaintext transit/decrypt/webapp-key ciphertext="$CIPHERTEXT" | base64 -d
echo
Subtask 3.4: Rotate the Encryption Key
vault write -f transit/keys/webapp-key/rotate
vault read transit/keys/webapp-key

# Confirm the previous ciphertext still decrypts correctly after rotation
vault write -field=plaintext transit/decrypt/webapp-key ciphertext="$CIPHERTEXT" | base64 -d
echo
Deliverable: the decrypted output after rotation still reads sensitive data, demonstrating that Vault retains prior key versions and can decrypt data encrypted before rotation.

Subtask 3.5: Apply a Hardened Configuration and Reconnect
sudo cp /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl.backup

cat > /tmp/vault-secure.hcl << 'EOF'
storage "file" {
  path = "/opt/vault/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address           = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable       = false
  tls_cert_file     = "/etc/vault.d/vault-cert.pem"
  tls_key_file      = "/etc/vault.d/vault-key.pem"
  tls_min_version   = "tls12"
  tls_cipher_suites = "TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256,TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA384"
}

api_addr     = "https://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"

ui = true
disable_mlock = true
log_level = "INFO"
log_file  = "/var/log/vault/vault.log"

default_lease_ttl = "1h"
max_lease_ttl     = "24h"
EOF

sudo cp /tmp/vault-secure.hcl /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl
sudo chown vault:vault /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl

sudo systemctl restart vault

# Wait for Vault to accept connections before proceeding
until vault status > /dev/null 2>&1; do
  echo "Waiting for Vault to start..."
  sleep 2
done

# A restart reseals Vault and any previous VAULT_TOKEN session is no longer valid
# against a sealed backend. Unseal and re-authenticate before continuing.
/tmp/unseal-vault.sh
export ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' /tmp/vault-init.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
export VAULT_TOKEN=$ROOT_TOKEN

vault status
Deliverable: after the restart, vault status reports Sealed: false, and a follow-up command such as vault policy list succeeds using the re-exported VAULT_TOKEN, confirming the session was correctly re-established.

Expected Outcomes
A running, TLS-enabled Vault instance with file-based audit logging enabled and generating real JSON audit entries
Distinct policies (admin, webapp, developer) correctly enforced for both userpass users and an AppRole-authenticated client
Working transit encryption with demonstrated key rotation and backward-compatible decryption
Troubleshooting
Symptom: Commands fail with Error checking seal status: Get "https://127.0.0.1:8200/v1/sys/seal-status": dial tcp ... connect: connection refused. Diagnostic hint: check sudo systemctl status vault --no-pager and sudo journalctl -u vault -n 50 to see if the service failed to start, often due to a config syntax error in /etc/vault.d/vault.hcl.
Symptom: A previously working vault command suddenly returns permission denied or * missing client token. Diagnostic hint: check whether Vault was restarted (which reseals it) or whether VAULT_TOKEN is still exported in your current shell session; re-run /tmp/unseal-vault.sh and re-export VAULT_TOKEN.
Conclusion
In this lab you deployed HashiCorp Vault with TLS enabled, initialized and unsealed it, and built a layered access-control model using both static users (userpass) and machine-oriented authentication (AppRole), verifying in each case that policies were actually enforced rather than just defined. You also enabled audit logging and inspected real audit entries, configured the transit secrets engine for encryption-as-a-service, and practiced key rotation while confirming backward compatibility with previously encrypted data. Finally, you experienced firsthand a common operational pitfall â that restarting Vault reseals it and invalidates in-shell sessions â and practiced the correct recovery procedure of unsealing and re-authenticating. These skills directly translate to operating production Vault clusters securely: enforcing least-privilege policies, verifying authentication boundaries before trusting them, and building repeatable scripts for unsealing, backup, and health checks rather than relying on manual, error-prone steps.
